# Database and Persistence Rules

- MySQL is the primary relational database defined for the platform.
- Migrations MUST be deterministic, reviewable, and reversible where practical.
- Use foreign keys and database constraints for critical integrity.
- Use appropriate indexes for status, ownership, proximity/tracking lookup, negotiation, and audit queries.
- Preserve historical financial values on the transaction record; never recompute old transactions from current configuration.
- Use UTC timestamps in persistence unless project infrastructure explicitly standardizes another storage rule.
- Use decimal/numeric-safe representations for money; never use floating point for monetary persistence.
- Avoid nullable columns when the domain requires a value; document legitimate null semantics.
- Add unique constraints for identifiers and idempotency keys that must be unique.
- Model one-to-many concepts such as DeliveryItems, Offers, CounterOffers, DeliveryEvents, DriverLocations, and attachments explicitly.
- Do not delete audit/financial history through cascading deletes.
- Use soft deletion only where the domain explicitly needs logical deletion.
- Database structure must support multiple active deliveries per driver.
